+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a string into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. It accepts either a whole number of
+seconds (`120`) or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups with the units `d`, `h`, `m` and `s`. Each unit may appear at
+most once, in that order, with no spaces, and units are case-insensitive: `2d`, `1h30m`, `45S`, `1d2h3m4s`, `90m`.
+Negative numbers, decimals and anything else are rejected.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration())
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(timeout)
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
